    "description": "How the global media and information ecosystem shapes international public opinion, domestic political constraints, and legitimacy of US operations. Modern conflict is fundamentally shaped by information warfare: social media distributes imagery of civilian casualties within minutes; state-aligned media (RT, PRESS TV, CCTV) amplifies counter-narratives; TikTok and Telegram carry unverified footage that influences publics faster than official statements. Key dynamics: (1) Iran operates a sophisticated information operation capability (2020 interference assessments, CISA); (2) Civilian casualty footage from precision strike 'collateral damage' rapidly drives international opposition; (3) US strategic communication capability has improved but remains reactive; (4) Israeli involvement amplifies Arab world opposition and creates coalition stress. Historical analog: 2003 Al Jazeera footage of civilian casualties dramatically shifted Arab opinion; 2023 Gaza hospital explosion demonstrates how mis-attribution drives narrative even when later corrected. 'Narrative dominance' operationalized as: whose framing prevails in G20 media, UN statements, and global protest levels.",
